EXT. FIELD KITCHEN - FORWARD CAMP - DAWN

A canvas lean-to against a ruined stone wall. A cauldron simmers over a fire. Mud, mist, and the distant percussion of artillery that has gone on so long it has become weather.

SERGEANT WILHELMINA OKORO-BRANDT (40s, flour on her sleeves) stirs a pot with a wooden paddle. PRIVATE LEOPOLDO ESCOBAR-TANAKA (19, helmet pushed back, mud to the knee) sits on an upturned bucket peeling potatoes. CORPORAL ABEBE TEKLE-NORDSTROM (30s, bandaged hand, cigarette behind one ear) leans on the wall with a tin cup.

Nobody speaks. A shell lands, far off. The pot shivers.

OKORO-BRANDT
More salt.

ESCOBAR-TANAKA
We're out.

OKORO-BRANDT
Then less water.

ESCOBAR-TANAKA
We're out of that too.

She keeps stirring. Tekle-Nordstrom takes the cigarette from behind his ear, looks at it, puts it back.

TEKLE-NORDSTROM
Rumor says the line moves tomorrow.

OKORO-BRANDT
Rumor says.

TEKLE-NORDSTROM
You don't believe it?

OKORO-BRANDT
I believe the potatoes.

Escobar-Tanaka drops a peel into a bucket. Picks up another potato. Does not peel it.

ESCOBAR-TANAKA
Sergeant.

OKORO-BRANDT
Private.

ESCOBAR-TANAKA
Is it all right if I write home?

OKORO-BRANDT
You may.

ESCOBAR-TANAKA
What do I say?

OKORO-BRANDT
That the soup's good.

ESCOBAR-TANAKA
Is it?

She tastes from the paddle. Chews. Considers.

OKORO-BRANDT
No.

Tekle-Nordstrom laughs once, quietly, into his cup. Escobar-Tanaka looks from one to the other and smiles for the first time in days. She hands him a bowl.

OKORO-BRANDT (CONT'D)
Eat.

He eats. A shell lands, closer. The pot rattles. Nobody looks up.
